1
00:00:01,330 --> 00:00:09,724
Welcome to Germany's states, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,382 --> 00:00:21,214
We work through 16 of the Germany regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:22,027 --> 00:00:25,603
Let's bring Schleswig-Holstein into view.

4
00:00:39,827 --> 00:00:50,357
Schleswig-Holstein fills the far north of Germany between Denmark and
Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, on both the Baltic and the North Sea, with Kiel on the
canal.

5
00:00:51,023 --> 00:01:01,983
Denmark ruled it until Prussia took it in 1864, and the border was only
settled by a plebiscite in 1920, which is why Danish schools survive in the
north.

6
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:12,699
Remember the very top of Germany, crossed by the Kiel Canal, the busiest
artificial waterway in the world, with more ships than Suez and Panama
together.

7
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:17,843
Let's bring Mecklenburg-Vorpommern into view.

8
00:01:31,064 --> 00:01:39,980
Mecklenburg-Vorpommern runs along the Baltic coast in the north-east, east of
Schleswig-Holstein, with Rostock as its largest city.

9
00:01:40,643 --> 00:01:48,445
It is the emptiest state in Germany, with fewer people to the square kilometre
than any other, and it is full of lakes.

10
00:01:49,151 --> 00:01:59,646
Remember the empty Land with its painted chalk cliffs, and with Rügen and
Usedom, Germany’s two largest islands, off the coast.

11
00:02:00,674 --> 00:02:02,717
Let's bring Hamburg into view.

12
00:02:22,437 --> 00:02:33,397
Hamburg is a small city-state on the Elbe in the north of Germany, far smaller
than the Länder around it, with Schleswig-Holstein above it and Niedersachsen
below.

13
00:02:34,055 --> 00:02:44,632
Its name comes from Hammaburg, a ninth-century castle, and it is officially
the Free and Hanseatic City, which still shows in its number plates, HH.

14
00:02:45,287 --> 00:02:52,961
Remember the small port city-state with more bridges than Venice, Amsterdam
and London put together.

15
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:55,979
Let's bring Bremen into view.

16
00:03:02,810 --> 00:03:16,846
Bremen is a tiny city-state in the north-west, far smaller than Niedersachsen
all around it, and it is really two towns, Bremen and Bremerhaven, with the
mainland in between.

17
00:03:17,498 --> 00:03:26,832
Its name comes from bremo, Old Saxon for the edge, because the town stood on
the bank of the Weser where the ground rose above the marsh.

18
00:03:27,483 --> 00:03:36,655
Remember the smallest Land, two separate towns with Lower Saxony in the gap,
and the statue of the town musicians who never actually got there.

19
00:03:37,381 --> 00:03:40,016
Let's bring Niedersachsen into view.

20
00:03:46,393 --> 00:03:58,293
Niedersachsen spreads across the whole north and north-west of Germany, from
the Dutch border to the Elbe, with the Wadden Sea along the coast and Hanover
in the middle.

21
00:03:58,948 --> 00:04:10,210
Lower means downriver, towards the sea, not southern, and the Saxons who gave
Sachsen its name started here before the name travelled south-east with a
duke’s title.

22
00:04:10,863 --> 00:04:17,295
Remember the biggest Land after Bayern and the flattest, where the tide
empties the Wadden Sea twice a day.

23
00:04:18,024 --> 00:04:19,893
Let's bring Berlin into view.

24
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:36,700
Berlin is a small city-state in the north-east of Germany, completely ringed
by Brandenburg and touching no other Land.

25
00:04:37,357 --> 00:04:44,218
It is really an island inside Brandenburg, and the two voted on merging in
1996 and said no.

26
00:04:44,868 --> 00:04:51,695
Remember the enclosed city-state that voted to stay on its own, with more
museums than rainy days in the year.

27
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:54,658
Let's bring Brandenburg into view.

28
00:05:03,277 --> 00:05:10,951
Brandenburg wraps all the way around Berlin in the north-east and reaches east
to the Oder, with Potsdam as its largest city.

29
00:05:11,609 --> 00:05:20,270
It is the cradle of Prussia, where the Margrave of Brandenburg became the King
in Prussia and everything from there to Königsberg followed.

30
00:05:20,961 --> 00:05:28,171
Remember the state that circles Berlin, with three thousand lakes that make it
the weekend of the whole capital.

31
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:31,710
Let's bring Sachsen-Anhalt into view.

32
00:05:38,157 --> 00:05:45,912
Sachsen-Anhalt lies in the centre of Germany between Brandenburg and
Thüringen, with Magdeburg on the Elbe.

33
00:05:46,563 --> 00:05:56,966
The state was only invented in 1945, joining Prussian Saxony to the old
principality of Anhalt, a name that sounds like the German for hold on.

34
00:05:57,626 --> 00:06:05,556
Remember the post-war state, with Luther nailing his theses in Wittenberg and
Bach born just over the border in Eisenach.

35
00:06:06,296 --> 00:06:09,059
Let's bring Nordrhein-Westfalen into view.

36
00:06:16,458 --> 00:06:27,673
Nordrhein-Westfalen fills the whole west of Germany, from the Netherlands and
Belgium down to the southern Rhineland, with Essen as its largest city and the
Ruhr around it.

37
00:06:28,324 --> 00:06:38,262
It is by far the most populous state, with one German in five living here and
a quarter of them in the Ruhr, whose coal and steel built modern Germany.

38
00:06:38,929 --> 00:06:46,650
Remember the crowded western Land of the Ruhr, where the last pit closed in
2018 after two centuries of mining.

39
00:06:47,374 --> 00:06:49,243
Let's bring Thüringen into view.

40
00:06:57,506 --> 00:07:08,292
Thüringen is the wooded state in the centre of Germany, between Hessen to the
west and Sachsen to the east, with Erfurt as its largest city.

41
00:07:08,943 --> 00:07:16,443
It is called the green heart of Germany, and it is the geographic centre of
the country as well as the wooded one.

42
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:23,625
Remember the green heart, where Weimar was the home of Goethe and Schiller and
the birthplace of the Bauhaus.

43
00:07:24,357 --> 00:07:26,656
Let's bring Sachsen into view.

44
00:07:41,187 --> 00:07:49,326
Sachsen is the large state in the east of Germany, reaching the Polish and
Czech borders, with Dresden as its largest city.

45
00:07:49,978 --> 00:08:01,576
The Saxons never actually lived here; the name arrived in 1423 with the
margraves of Meissen, six hundred kilometres away from where the Saxons really
were.

46
00:08:02,237 --> 00:08:09,609
Remember the Land where the first true porcelain outside China was fired at
Meissen in 1708.

47
00:08:10,451 --> 00:08:12,622
Let's bring Hessen into view.

48
00:08:28,098 --> 00:08:39,395
Hessen sits in the centre of Germany and touches six other Länder, including
Rheinland-Pfalz to the west and Bayern to the south, with Frankfurt as its
largest city.

49
00:08:40,052 --> 00:08:47,088
Its name comes from the Chatti, a tribe Tacitus wrote about, and Chatti became
Hessi and then Hessen.

50
00:08:47,744 --> 00:08:56,277
Remember the middle state whose landgraves rented soldiers to Britain in the
1770s, so American schoolchildren learn the word Hessians.

51
00:08:57,043 --> 00:08:59,771
Let's bring Rheinland-Pfalz into view.

52
00:09:14,961 --> 00:09:26,559
Rheinland-Pfalz lies along the Rhine and the Moselle in the far west of
Germany, with Koblenz as its largest city and Saarland tucked into its
south-west.

53
00:09:27,218 --> 00:09:37,458
The Pfalz is Latin palatium, a palace, because the Count Palatine was the
emperor’s deputy and his lands kept the title.

54
00:09:38,114 --> 00:09:51,082
Remember the wine state, which grows two thirds of German wine, and Mainz,
where Gutenberg printed the first book with movable type around 1450.

55
00:09:51,986 --> 00:09:54,204
Let's bring Saarland into view.

56
00:10:06,942 --> 00:10:19,435
Saarland is the small state in the far south-west of Germany, bounded by
Rheinland-Pfalz on the north and east and by France and Luxembourg on the west
and south, with Saarbrücken as its largest city.

57
00:10:20,090 --> 00:10:33,569
It voted twice on its own future, in 1935 to rejoin Germany and in 1955
against independence, so it joined West Germany in 1957 as the very last
piece.

58
00:10:34,225 --> 00:10:43,141
Remember the last piece to arrive, the smallest Land apart from the
city-states, where the Saar loops back on itself at Mettlach.

59
00:10:43,897 --> 00:10:45,766
Let's bring Bayern into view.

60
00:10:56,350 --> 00:11:04,280
Bayern is the biggest state by area, filling the south of Germany from the
Alps up past Munich, which is its largest city.

61
00:11:04,973 --> 00:11:21,436
It is the oldest state here, a duchy from 555 and a kingdom from 1806, and it
joined the German Empire in 1871 while keeping its own army and postal
service.

62
00:11:22,087 --> 00:11:31,770
Remember the great southern Land, which holds half of all German ground above
a thousand metres, and Neuschwanstein, the model for Disney’s castle.

63
00:11:33,488 --> 00:11:36,634
Let's bring Baden-Württemberg into view.

64
00:11:51,589 --> 00:12:02,166
Baden-Württemberg is the south-western state, running from the Black Forest in
the west across the Swabian Jura, with Stuttgart as its largest city.

65
00:12:02,836 --> 00:12:12,728
The two names were welded together in 1952, and Baden is Old High German for
baths, after the Roman springs at Baden-Baden.

66
00:12:13,389 --> 00:12:24,813
Remember the merged Land of 1952, where the Danube rises in the Black Forest
and Mercedes and Porsche are both built around Stuttgart.

67
00:12:41,900 --> 00:13:03,428
16 regions of Germany, one for each target of the map: Schleswig-Holstein,
Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Hamburg, Bremen, Niedersachsen, Berlin, Brandenburg,
Sachsen-Anhalt, Nordrhein-Westfalen, Thüringen, Sachsen, Hessen,
Rheinland-Pfalz, Saarland, Bayern and Baden-Württemberg.

68
00:13:04,099 --> 00:13:13,817
Remember the shape of Germany from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
